## Data Model Guidance

Give the AI tool this minimum schema to start from (expand as needed):

- `users` (id, email, password_hash, role, employee_id)
- `employees` (id, name, department_id, designation, manager_id, date_of_joining, status)
- `departments` (id, name)
- `leave_types` (id, name, days_per_year, carry_forward_allowed)
- `leave_balances` (employee_id, leave_type_id, balance, year)
- `leave_requests` (id, employee_id, leave_type_id, start_date, end_date, reason, status, approver_id)
- `attendance` (id, employee_id, date, status, check_in, check_out)
- `payroll_runs` (id, month, year, status)
- `payslips` (id, employee_id, payroll_run_id, gross, deductions, net, pdf_url)

## Non-Functional Requirements

- Responsive UI (must work well on mobile for employee self-service)
- All list views must support pagination — don't load full tables into the frontend
- Every write action (leave approval, payroll run) must be logged with actor + timestamp
- Environment variables for all secrets — no hardcoded credentials
- Seed script with sample data (5-10 employees, a few departments) so the app is testable immediately after setup

## Build Order (tell the AI tool to follow this sequence)

1. Auth + roles + employee CRUD
2. Leave management end-to-end (apply → approve → balance update)
3. Attendance tracking
4. Payroll (basic)
5. Recruitment + Performance (only if time/scope allows)
6. Reports/dashboard last, once data exists to visualize

